      "output": {
        "message": "Here are three practical directions you could try, depending on what you want from your evenings: 1. **Establish a regular \"third place\":** Pick a local spot—a coffee shop, gym, library, or neighborhood park—and go at roughly the same time once or twice a week. It gets you out among people with zero pressure to strike up a conversation right away.\n2. **Join an activity-based group:** Look for local meetups centered on something concrete, like a casual running or walking club, board game nights, a rec sports league, or a weekly volunteer shift. Doing an activity together makes talking to new people much easier than going to a purely social mixer.\n3. **Give solo evenings an intentional structure:** If you prefer staying in or need to recharge, plan the time around a specific activity—like exploring a new neighborhood on the way home, cooking a new recipe, or working on a distinct project—so the evening feels deliberate rather than just empty. Which of those sounds closest to what you want right now?",
